717 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265704 1080 #uuid "fe108dd5-5b35-596c-94e7-349c53f9ba7a" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265705 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263053 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265705 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263218 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265705 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264004 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265705 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264382 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265705 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264545 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265705 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265705 1077 "selection" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265705 1079 #edb/ref 17592186264382 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265705 1080 #uuid "fe4109b4-3f1c-5dbf-9f78-c5a2749d9808" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265706 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263180 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265706 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263368 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265706 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264350 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265706 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264703 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265706 1075 #edb/ref 17592186265070 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265706 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265706 1077 "selection" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265706 1079 #edb/ref 17592186265070 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265706 1080 #uuid "fe4c3e9c-7bc4-5ef2-a04f-d6700d9680d6" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265707 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263166 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265707 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264108 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265707 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264469 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265707 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264936 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265707 1075 #edb/ref 17592186265234 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265707 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265707 1077 "selection" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265707 1079 #edb/ref 17592186265234 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265707 1080 #uuid "feb896cd-4c64-57b3-b41b-09de07f30699" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265708 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263072 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265708 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263839 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265708 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263921 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265708 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263974 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265708 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264518 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265708 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265708 1077 "selection" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265708 1079 #edb/ref 17592186263974 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265708 1080 #uuid "ffd7877a-8c6a-5304-9c7d-554d32704288" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265709 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265405 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265709 1069 "q-129260" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265709 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265709 1071 "Given the two planes $3x+y-2z=0$ and $2x+y+3z+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨5,-13,1⟩.$\n- The planes are parallel.\n- The planes intersect on a line." 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265709 1072 #uuid "999cfdc7-ea79-5a7b-bd15-dce6f8b9e282" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265709 1074 "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x+y-2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,1,-2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x+y+3z+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,1,3⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,1,-2⟩\\times ⟨2,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =5i-13j+k \\\\  & =⟨5,-13,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the corresponding planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only I and III only are true." 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265710 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265528 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265710 1069 "q-129261" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265710 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265710 1071 "Given the two planes $x-y+2z=0$ and $5x+y-13=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨-3,12,6⟩.$\n- The planes intersect on a line.\n- The planes are coincident." 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265710 1072 #uuid "d9644136-e70e-5412-95e4-ddf24de80c84" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265710 1074 "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-1,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $5x+y-13=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-1,2⟩\\times ⟨5,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-2i+10j+6k \\\\  & =⟨-2,10,6⟩\\ne ⟨-3,12,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the corresponding planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes could not be coincident either.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265711 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265657 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265711 1069 "q-129268" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265711 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265711 1071 "Calculate \n${\\int }_{4}^{0}f(t)+⟨3,\\,1,\\,-2⟩\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{0}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-3,\\,6,\\,0⟩.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265711 1072 #uuid "620fd628-6fe2-5040-9cd0-aaf37f31206b" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265711 1074 "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-⟨-3,\\,6,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,-6,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the vector addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{0}f(t)+⟨3,\\,1,\\,-2⟩\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{4}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{0}⟨3,\\,1,\\,-2⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,-6,\\,0⟩+⟨3t,\\,t,\\,-2t⟩{|}_{4}^{0} \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,-6,\\,0⟩+⟨-12,\\,-4,\\,8⟩\\text{ } \\\\  & =⟨-9,\\,-10,\\,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265712 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265537 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265712 1069 "q-129270" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265712 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265712 1071 "Calculate \n${\\int }_{0}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{0}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨5,\\,1,\\,20⟩$\nand\n${\\int }_{4}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨1,\\,1,\\,8⟩.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265712 1072 #uuid "59ecc43a-abb8-5e91-8373-1f5a059e68df" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265712 1074 "Using the adjacent intervals rule, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{4}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nFinally, using the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{4}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨5,\\,1,\\,20⟩-⟨1,\\,1,\\,8⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,0,\\,12⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265713 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265672 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265713 1069 "q-129275" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265713 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265713 1071 "Calculate \n${\\int }_{3}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{5}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-4,\\,-12,\\,14⟩$\nand\n${\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨2,\\,8,\\,-8⟩.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265713 1072 #uuid "1a43ebb2-ac19-5078-b7f7-001baec340ee" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265713 1074 "Using the adjacent intervals rule, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nFinally, using the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{5}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-⟨-4,\\,-12,\\,14⟩-⟨2,\\,8,\\,-8⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,12,\\,-14⟩-⟨2,\\,8,\\,-8⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,4,\\,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265714 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265623 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265714 1069 "q-129287" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265714 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265714 1071 "Given that \n${\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=4\\,i-2\\,j+k,$ \n$c=u\\,i-2\\,j-2\\,k,$\nand \n${\\int }_{0}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0,$ what is the value of $u?$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265714 1072 #uuid "ba256482-462a-5d75-b507-93da6ae329ed" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265714 1074 "The dot product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the dot-product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =c\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(u\\,i-2\\,j-2\\,k)\\cdot (4\\,i-2\\,j+\\,k) \\\\  & =4u+4-2 \\\\  & =4u+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${\\int }_{0}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0,$ we must have $4u+2=0,$ which means that $u=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265715 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265513 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265715 1069 "q-129292" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265715 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265715 1071 "Find ${\\int }_{1}^{4}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ \ngiven that \n${\\int }_{1}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t={e}^{2}\\,i-\\,j+e\\,k$ \nand \n$c={e}^{-1}\\,i+j+k.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265715 1072 #uuid "b0c6f288-8211-53d5-9b2d-97546a2976b7" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265715 1074 "The dot product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the dot-product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =c\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({e}^{-1}\\,i+j+k)\\cdot ({e}^{2}\\,i-\\,j+e\\,k) \\\\  & =e-1+e \\\\  & =2e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265716 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265603 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265716 1069 "q-129295" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265716 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265716 1071 "Find ${\\int }_{0}^{3}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ \ngiven that \n${\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=i+j-3\\,k$ \nand \n$c=i-j+2\\,k.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265716 1072 #uuid "553c9eff-921e-5636-9757-6a75aa064098" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265716 1074 "The cross-product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\times {\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the cross-product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =c\\times {\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(i-j+2\\,k)\\times (i+j-3\\,k) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(3-2)i-(-3-2)j+(1+1)k \\\\  & =i+5\\,j+2\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265717 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265355 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265717 1069 "q-129322" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265717 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265717 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265717 1072 #uuid "2fa0e8d7-3133-5471-9c08-501132fe188f" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265717 1074 "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(0,-1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting $(0,-1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}-(-1)^{2}}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-1}{0+1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265718 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265421 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265718 1069 "q-129329" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265718 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265718 1071 "Determine ${lim}_{x\\to (1,-2)}\\sqrt[3]{3x-2y+1}.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265718 1072 #uuid "d53fcb5b-03e0-54f4-94bd-b9eac7281e74" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265718 1074 "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{3x-2y+1}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(1,-2)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,-2)}\\sqrt[3]{3x-2y+1} & =\\sqrt[3]{3(1)-2(-2)+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{3+4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265719 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265442 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265719 1069 "q-129332" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265719 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265719 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{xz+yz}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265719 1072 #uuid "8d67ac27-26e1-5580-9eb2-034565720f25" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265719 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{xz+yz}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{(-1)(2)+1(2)}{(-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2+2}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{xz+yz}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{z(x+y)}{(x+y)(x-y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{z\\cancel{(x+y)}}{\\cancel{(x+y)}(x-y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{z}{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{z}{x-y} & =\\frac{2}{-1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-1.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265332 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265720 1069 "q-129335" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265720 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265720 1071 "Determine ${lim}_{x\\to (2,2)}\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+3x{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-2xy+{y}^{2}}.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265720 1072 #uuid "715f6fef-c679-5b35-a9f5-f8ac13307bba" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265720 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,2)}\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+3x{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-2xy+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{{2}^{3}-3({2}^{2})(2)+3(2)({2}^{2})-{2}^{3}}{{2}^{2}-2(2)(2)+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-24+24-8}{4-8+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,2)}\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+3x{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-2xy+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,2)}\\frac{(x-y)^{3}}{(x-y)^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,2)}\\frac{(x-y)(x-y)^{2}}{(x-y)^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,2)}\\frac{(x-y)\\cancel{(x-y)^{2}}}{\\cancel{(x-y)^{2}}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,2)}(x-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,2)}(x-y) & =2-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265721 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265547 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265721 1069 "q-129339" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265721 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265721 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy}.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265721 1072 #uuid "aa92e0c7-08cd-5cb2-af58-76718486c28e" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265721 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}-(-1)^{2}}{(-1)^{2}-(-1)(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy} & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{(x+y)(x-y)}{x(x-y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{(x+y)\\cancel{(x-y)}}{x\\cancel{(x-y)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{x+y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{x+y}{x} & =\\frac{-1+(-1)}{(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)}{(-1)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $2.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265722 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265480 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265722 1069 "q-129342" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265722 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265722 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}+x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}}=$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265722 1072 #uuid "d8bc5519-0c3a-5438-9140-d93c11fd7b22" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265722 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}+x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{0}-\\sqrt{0}+0-0}{\\sqrt{0}-\\sqrt{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0+0-0}{0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}+x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}+x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})+(x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{x-y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{x-y+(x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{x-y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{(x-y)(1+\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{x-y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-y)}(1+\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{\\cancel{x-y}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}(1+\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}(1+\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}) & =1+\\sqrt{0}+\\sqrt{0} \\\\  & =1+0+0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $1.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265723 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265692 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265723 1069 "q-129345" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265723 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265723 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{x\\sqrt{y+z}-1}{{x}^{2}(y+z)-1}=$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265723 1072 #uuid "43a8fc7d-552c-5ee1-ac4a-431ddf37f045" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265723 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{x\\sqrt{y+z}-1}{{x}^{2}(y+z)-1} & =\\frac{1\\sqrt{2+(-1)}-1}{({1}^{2})(2+(-1))-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{x\\sqrt{y+z}-1}{{x}^{2}(y+z)-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{(x\\sqrt{y+z}-1)(x\\sqrt{y+z}+1)}{({x}^{2}(y+z)-1)(x\\sqrt{y+z}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}(y+z)-1}{({x}^{2}(y+z)-1)(x\\sqrt{y+z}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}(y+z)-1}}{\\cancel{({x}^{2}(y+z)-1)}(x\\sqrt{y+z}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{y+z}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{y+z}+1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\sqrt{2+(-1)}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265724 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265335 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265724 1069 "q-129346" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265724 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265724 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{x+y-{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x+y}-z}.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265724 1072 #uuid "98eb7cb6-6c4e-52ce-9f7d-3ac96c9eabeb" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265724 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{x+y-{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x+y}-z} & =\\frac{1+0-{1}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1+0}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+0-1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{x+y-{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x+y}-z} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{(x+y-{z}^{2})(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)}{(\\sqrt{x+y}-z)(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{(x+y-{z}^{2})(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)}{x+y-{z}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{\\cancel{(x+y-{z}^{2})}(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)}{\\cancel{x+y-{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}(\\sqrt{x+y}+z) & =\\sqrt{1+0}+1 \\\\  & =1+0+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $2.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265725 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265539 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265725 1069 "q-129348" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265725 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265725 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}-3}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}=$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265725 1072 #uuid "b38eaadb-c5b0-5008-b859-4e13e1a65105" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265725 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}-3}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{({0}^{2})({0}^{2})+9}-3}{({0}^{2})({0}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-3}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}-3}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}-3)(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3)}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9-9}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{({0}^{2})({0}^{2})+9}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{6}.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265726 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265544 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265726 1069 "q-129352" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265726 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265726 1071 "Let $T=UV.$ What is the value of ${t}_{21}$, if\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265726 1072 #uuid "311db904-8f85-528c-932d-d35df8452fa9" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265726 1074 "Multiplying the $2$nd row in $U$ by the $1$st column in $V$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{21} & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =10+12 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265727 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265301 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265727 1069 "q-129405" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265727 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265727 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\n- The limit evaluates to $2$ along the path $y=0$\n- The limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{3}$ along the path $y=x$\n- The limit does not exist" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265727 1072 #uuid "32eaab66-626d-536b-9465-8d972be98972" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265727 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2({0}^{2})-{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+2({0}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2({0}^{2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is also true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{3\\cancel{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265728 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265636 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265728 1069 "q-129406" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265728 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265728 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\n- The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=0$\n- The limit evaluates to $\\frac{2}{3}$ along the path $y=x$\n- The limit does not exist" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265728 1072 #uuid "88abe9a3-7bd6-5ff9-8f3e-e809f8b5b178" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265728 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2(0)(0)}{3({0}^{2})-{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2x(0)}{3({0}^{2})-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x(x)}{3{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265729 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265665 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265729 1069 "q-129408" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265729 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265729 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\n- The limit evaluates to $4$ along the path $y={x}^{4}$\n- The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $x=0$\n- The limit does not exist" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265729 1072 #uuid "7bd92abc-0899-50b8-83be-62e32b920d95" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265729 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2({0}^{2})\\sqrt{0}}{{0}^{4}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{4}}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{4}}}{{x}^{4}+({x}^{4})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{8}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}(1+{x}^{4})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2\\cancel{{x}^{4}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{4}}(1+{x}^{4})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+{0}^{4}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,x=0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{2({0}^{2})\\sqrt{y}}{{0}^{4}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265730 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265506 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265730 1069 "q-129423" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265730 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265730 1071 "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265730 1072 #uuid "3458fbf5-87c4-5bfc-9974-f949a6151b73" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265730 1074 "Notice that by replacing ${a}_{22}$, ${a}_{33}$ and ${a}_{44}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\,⟶\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe other options will not give us a matrix in row echelon form." 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265731 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265259 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265731 1069 "q-129424" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265731 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265731 1071 "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265731 1072 #uuid "4da5d683-7056-5520-8077-4c10803e9f98" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265731 1074 "Notice that by replacing ${a}_{31}$, ${a}_{32}$ and ${a}_{43}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\,⟶\\,[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe other options will not give us a matrix in row echelon form." 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265732 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265515 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265732 1069 "q-129426" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265732 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265732 1071 "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]=$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265732 1072 #uuid "04f480dc-232f-565e-ae13-87a295ba14b9" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265732 1074 "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0-6+15 & 2+3+0 & 4-9-9 \\\\ 0+0-5 & -12+0+0 & -24-0+3 \\\\ 0-2+10 & 0+1+0 & 0-3-6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 5 & -14 \\\\ -5 & -12 & -21 \\\\ 8 & 1 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265733 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265628 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265733 1069 "q-129437" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265733 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265733 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,S=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the matrices $P,R,$ and $S?$\n\n- The product $RS$ is well defined\n- The product $RP$ is well defined\n- The product $SP$ is well defined" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265733 1072 #uuid "22b7c2e7-9621-5013-a411-a722a7ceea01" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265733 1074 "Notice that $P$ is a $5\\times 3$ matrix, $R$ is a $1\\times 3$ row vector, and $S$ is a $1\\times 5$ row vector. \n\nThere are $3$ columns in $R$ and $1$ row in $S.$ Since the number of columns in $R$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $S,$ the matrices $R$ and $S$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{R}_{1\\times 3}{\\times }_{\\ne }{S}_{1\\times 5}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $3$ columns in $R$ and $5$ rows in $P.$ Since the number of columns in $R$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $P,$ the matrices $R$ and $P$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{R}_{1\\times 3}{\\times }_{\\ne }{P}_{5\\times 3}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $5$ columns in $S$ and $5$ rows in $P.$ Since the number of columns in $S$ equals the number of rows in $P,$ the matrices $S$ and $P$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{S}_{1\\times \\boxed{\\text{5}}}{\\times }_{✓}{P}_{\\boxed{\\text{5}}\\times 3}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nTherefore, statement III is true, while statements I and II are false." 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265734 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265396 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265734 1069 "q-129439" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265734 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265734 1071 "Determine the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+3$ at the point $(0,1,1).$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265734 1072 #uuid "c9c6a200-53f4-5ee2-ac37-189c11ad9a9e" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265734 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+3) \\\\  & =2x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+3) \\\\  & =-4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y)=(0,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,1) & =2(0)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,1) & =-4(1)=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-1 & =(x-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,1)+(y-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,1) \\\\ z-1 & =0x-4(y-1) \\\\ z-1 & =-4y+4 \\\\ 4y+z-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265735 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265423 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265735 1069 "q-129447" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265735 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265735 1071 "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=2 \\\\  & {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & {x}_{3}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265735 1072 #uuid "b7e933dd-83fd-54e7-932e-d388f7f7060c" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265735 1074 "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(-1)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}+1=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${x}_{3}=-1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(\\frac{1}{2})+(-1)=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2+1+1=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=4$, ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}$, and ${x}_{3}=-1.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨4,\\frac{1}{2},-1⟩.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265736 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265474 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265736 1069 "q-129452" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265736 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265736 1071 "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+y-z=2 \\\\  & y-z=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & z=3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265736 1072 #uuid "ffbc1914-8f55-58fc-9939-7cf601faf78b" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265736 1074 "The third equation states that $z=3.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny-3=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}+3=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $y=\\frac{7}{2}$ and $z=3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+\\frac{7}{2}-3=2\\,⟹\\,x=2-\\frac{7}{2}+3=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\frac{3}{2}$, $y=\\frac{7}{2}$, and $z=3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265737 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265414 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265737 1069 "q-129453" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265737 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265737 1071 "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=4 \\\\  & {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=2 \\\\  & {x}_{3}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265737 1072 #uuid "3719eef8-ec9e-5d13-b3ad-dc6ee7926695" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265737 1074 "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=-2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(-2)=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=2+2=4.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{2}=4$ and ${x}_{3}=-2$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-4-(-2)=4\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=4+4-2=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=6$, ${x}_{2}=4$, and ${x}_{3}=-2.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨6,4,-2⟩.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265738 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265559 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265738 1069 "q-129455" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265738 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265738 1071 "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=2 \\\\  & {x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}=3 \\\\  & {x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265738 1072 #uuid "bdb97edb-fdd8-52f8-9de8-da5dc252cf96" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265738 1074 "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-6(-\\frac{1}{3})=3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=3-2=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+1-(-\\frac{1}{3})=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2-1-\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}$, ${x}_{2}=1$, and ${x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265739 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265303 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265739 1069 "q-129457" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265739 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265739 1071 "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{3} & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265739 1072 #uuid "8c3fa01d-ccbd-5a15-bdb2-2085c84ec955" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265739 1074 "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say,  ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=2 \\\\  & {x}_{2}+\\frac{{x}_{3}}{3}=-1 \\\\  & {x}_{3}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that ${x}_{3}=2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+\\frac{2}{3}=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-1-\\frac{2}{3}=-\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{3}$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+3(-\\frac{5}{3})+(2)=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2+5-2=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=5$, ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{3}$, and ${x}_{3}=2.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨5,-\\frac{5}{3},2⟩.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265740 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265577 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265740 1069 "q-129459" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265740 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265740 1071 "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265740 1072 #uuid "b219ecfb-921e-5b50-978c-98cd2c9acabd" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265740 1074 "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+y+3z=-2 \\\\  & y+4z=0 \\\\  & z=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that $z=3.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+4(3)=0\\,⟹\\,y=-12.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=3$ and $y=-12$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+(-12)+3(3)=-2\\,⟹\\,x=-2+12-9=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=1$, $y=-12$, and $z=3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨1,-12,3⟩.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265741 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265707 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265741 1069 "q-129461" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265741 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265741 1071 "Suppose $A$ is a $4\\times 6$ matrix and $B$ is a  $2\\times 4$ matrix. What are the dimensions of $BA?$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265741 1072 #uuid "fe7c5fa2-862d-5a34-a530-b3d3ed2694e7" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265741 1074 "There are $4$ columns in $B$ and $4$ rows in $A.$ Since the number of columns in $B$ equals the number of rows in $A,$ the matrices $B$ and $A$ are conformable for multiplication, and $BA$ is a $2\\times 6$ matrix:\n\n$$\n{B}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{4}}}{\\times }_{✓}{A}_{\\boxed{\\text{4}}\\times 6}={BA}_{2\\times 6}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265742 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265409 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265742 1069 "q-129476" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265742 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265742 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}-\\sqrt{z}$ at the point $(1,9,4,2).$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265742 1072 #uuid "ba77fa6c-6a35-5c61-ab53-8720f1df1a35" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265742 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}-\\sqrt{z})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}-\\sqrt{z})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}-\\sqrt{z})=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(1,9,4):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,9,4) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,9,4) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{9}}=\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,9,4) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-2 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,9,4)+(y-9)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,9,4)+(z-4)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,9,4) \\\\ w-2 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)+\\frac{1}{6}(y-9)-\\frac{1}{4}(z-4) \\\\ w-2 & =\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{6}y-\\frac{1}{4}z-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{6}y-\\frac{1}{4}z-w+1 & =0 \\\\ 12(\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{6}y-\\frac{1}{4}z-w+1) & =12\\cdot 0 \\\\ 6x+2y-3z-12w+12 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265743 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265333 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265743 1069 "q-129481" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265743 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265743 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=z\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)$ at the point $(0,0,3,0).$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265743 1072 #uuid "53a77fe1-eae0-5bd2-80a2-9ca9416db144" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265743 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1))=\\frac{2xz}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1))=\\frac{2yz}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(0,0,3):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,0,3) & =\\frac{2(0)(3)}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+1}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,0,3) & =\\frac{2(0)(3)}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+1}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(0,0,3) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-0 & =(x-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,0,3)+(y-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,0,3)+(z-3)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(0,0,3) \\\\ w & =0x+0y+0(z-3) \\\\ w & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265744 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265575 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265744 1069 "q-129483" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265744 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265744 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=z\\sqrt{y}-x$ at the point $(-1,4,1,3).$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265744 1072 #uuid "152fa2a1-ccb2-5b4a-abda-500e8681371b" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265744 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z\\sqrt{y}-x)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z\\sqrt{y}-x)=\\frac{z}{2\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z\\sqrt{y}-x)=\\sqrt{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(-1,4,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(-1,4,1) & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(-1,4,1) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(-1,4,1) & =\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-3 & =(x-(-1))\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(-1,4,1)+(y-4)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(-1,4,1)+(z-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(-1,4,1) \\\\ w-3 & =-(x+1)+\\frac{1}{4}(y-4)+2(z-1) \\\\ w-3 & =-x+\\frac{1}{4}y+2z-4 \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{4}y-2z+w+1 & =0 \\\\ 4x-y-8z+4w+4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265745 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265546 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265745 1069 "q-129485" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265745 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265745 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=4x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(yz)$ at the point $(-1,1,1,-\\pi ).$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265745 1072 #uuid "d1621000-820d-5620-bf58-3ce548df1dda" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265745 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(yz))=4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(yz) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(yz))=\\frac{4xz}{1+{y}^{2}{z}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(yz))=\\frac{4xy}{1+{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(-1,1,1) & =4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1\\cdot 1)=\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(-1,1,1) & =\\frac{4(-1)(1)}{1+({1}^{2})({1}^{2})}=-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(-1,1,1) & =\\frac{4(-1)(1)}{1+({1}^{2})({1}^{2})}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-(-\\pi ) & =(x-(-1))\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(-1,1,1)+(y-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(-1,1,1)+(z-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(-1,1,1) \\\\ w+\\pi  & =\\pi (x+1)-2(y-1)-2(z-1) \\\\ w+\\pi  & =\\pi x-2y-2z+4+\\pi  \\\\ \\pi x-2y-2z-w+4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265746 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265695 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265746 1069 "q-129486" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265746 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265746 1071 "Suppose $A$ is a $8\\times 7$ matrix and $B$ is a  $2\\times 8$ matrix. What are the dimensions of $BA?$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265746 1072 #uuid "cf9309d5-2231-52e7-ae7b-f1293b0c5bdd" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265746 1074 "There are $8$ columns in $B$ and $8$ rows in $A.$ Since the number of columns in $B$ equals the number of rows in $A,$ the matrices $B$ and $A$ are conformable for multiplication, and $BA$ is a $2\\times 7$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{B}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{8}}}{\\times }_{✓}{A}_{\\boxed{\\text{8}}\\times 7}={BA}_{2\\times 7}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265747 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265426 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265747 1069 "q-129489" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265747 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265747 1071 "Let\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 7 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 8 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}].$\nWhat are the dimensions of $BA?$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265747 1072 #uuid "7f2cfa96-3e65-501e-ab18-cce696d48042" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265747 1074 "We notice that $A$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix and $B$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix. \n\nThere are $3$ columns in $B$ and $3$ rows in $A.$ Since the number of columns in $B$ equals the number of rows in $A,$ the matrices $B$ and $A$ are conformable for multiplication. \n\nThe product $BA$ has dimensions $2\\times 4:$\n\n$$\n{B}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\times }_{✓}{A}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 4}={BA}_{2\\times 4}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265748 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265417 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265748 1069 "q-129492" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265748 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265748 1071 "Let\n$C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -5 & -7\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -5 \\\\ -3 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].$\nWhat are the dimensions of $CD?$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265748 1072 #uuid "af3c60eb-8137-5aff-ac44-7e1785bdc22b" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265748 1074 "We notice that $C$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $D$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $3$ columns in $C$ and $3$ rows in $D.$ Since the number of columns in $C$ equals the number of rows in $D,$ the matrices $C$ and $D$ are conformable for multiplication. \n\nThe product $CD$ has dimensions $3\\times 2:$ \n\n$$\n{C}_{3\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\times }_{✓}{D}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 2}={CD}_{3\\times 2}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265749 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265473 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265749 1069 "q-129495" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265749 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265749 1071 "Find $AB$, if\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265749 1072 #uuid "78f7d23e-60ed-507c-b4f6-6101bce65778" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265749 1074 "Here, $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix and $B$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $3$ columns in $A$ and $3$ rows in $B,$ so $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 2}={AB}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $AB,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-15 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}], &  & \\,-15=2\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot 6+1\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-15 & 3 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}], &  & \\,3=2\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 5+1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-15 & 3 \\\\ -9 & ∗\\end{aligned}], &  & \\,-9=1\\cdot (-3)+0\\cdot 6+2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-15 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 10\\end{aligned}], &  & \\,10=1\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$AB=[\\begin{aligned}-15 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 10\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265750 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265508 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265750 1069 "q-129496" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265750 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265750 1071 "Find $XY$,  if  $X=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $Y=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265750 1072 #uuid "910b9da2-b0da-578c-b6de-5df3df9fdc3f" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265750 1074 "Here, $X$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix and $Y$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $3$ columns in $X$ and $3$ rows in $Y,$ so $X$ and $Y$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{X}_{1\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{Y}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 2}={XY}_{1\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $XY,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}22 & ∗\\end{aligned}], &  & \\,22=5\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-2)+4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & [\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}22 & 9\\end{aligned}], &  & \\,9=5\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 1+4\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$XY=[\\begin{aligned}22 & 9\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265751 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265597 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265751 1069 "q-129549" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265751 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265751 1071 "${\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265751 1072 #uuid "36d30b73-a8de-5a80-801a-a56d384cf14e" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265751 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant. \n\nMaking a substitution $x+y=u,$ $\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u,$ we get.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{y}^{1+y}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[-\\frac{1}{u}]}_{y}^{1+y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{y+1}+\\frac{1}{y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\frac{1}{y}-\\frac{1}{y+1}]\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\frac{1}{y}-\\frac{1}{y+1}]\\,\\text{d}y & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{y}{y+1}){|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265752 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265375 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265752 1069 "q-129550" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265752 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265752 1071 "${\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265752 1072 #uuid "9401f001-e9d0-5065-a0ef-28a934cd8591" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265752 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}y[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}y{[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]}_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}y[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265753 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265388 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265753 1069 "q-129554" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265753 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265753 1071 "At which points on the surface $z={x}^{3}+xy$ is the tangent plane horizontal?" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265753 1072 #uuid "614f0c59-2007-5d79-93a8-aaee2f0fc248" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265753 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+xy)=3{x}^{2}+y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+xy)=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨3{x}^{2}+y,\\,x,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+y=0 \\\\ x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the second equation we get $x=0.$ Substituting $x=0$ into first equation, we obtain $y=0.$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz={0}^{3}+0(0)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,0).$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265754 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265354 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265754 1069 "q-129557" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265754 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265754 1071 "At which point on the surface $z=3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector  $v=⟨4,-2,-1⟩?$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265754 1072 #uuid "12830aeb-cb43-530e-902c-e52ba9d7524f" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265754 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=6x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨6x,\\,2y,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨4,-2,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨6x,\\,2y,\\,-1⟩=⟨4,-2,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6cx=4 \\\\ 2cy=-2 \\\\ -c=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n6(1)x=4\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n2(1)y=-2\\,⟹\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $y=-1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =3{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}+(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector $v$ at $(\\frac{2}{3},-1,\\frac{7}{3}).$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265755 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265482 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265755 1069 "q-129569" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265755 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265755 1071 "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265755 1072 #uuid "a301e776-bc09-550c-bf21-25438dba0f53" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265755 1074 "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say,  ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\  & {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=3 \\\\  & {x}_{3}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(-1)=3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=3+1=4.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=4$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(4)+3(-1)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=8+3=11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=11$, ${x}_{2}=4$, and ${x}_{3}=-1.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨11,4,-1⟩.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265756 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265693 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265756 1069 "q-129599" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265756 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265756 1071 "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265756 1072 #uuid "f98c00cd-fa7a-5960-9ffe-f0175b73e871" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265756 1074 "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+y+2z=-1 \\\\  & y+3z=2 \\\\  & z=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that $z=3.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+3(3)=2\\,⟹\\,y=2-9=-7.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=3$ and $y=-7$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+(-7)+2(3)=-1\\,⟹\\,x=-1+7-6=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=0$, $y=-7$, and $z=3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨0,-7,3⟩.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265757 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265498 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265757 1069 "q-129604" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265757 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265757 1071 "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265757 1072 #uuid "767893ce-f15e-5dc3-ac2a-90c7bba1c030" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265757 1074 "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 4x+y+2z=-3 \\\\  & y+3z=0 \\\\  & -z=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that \n\n$$\n-z=1\\,⟹\\,z=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+3(-1)=0\\,⟹\\,y=3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=-1$ and $y=3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n4x+(3)+2(-1)=-3\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{-3-3+2}{4}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-1$, $y=3$, and $z=-1.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as \n$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265758 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265384 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265758 1069 "q-129613" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265758 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265758 1071 "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=-1 \\\\  & {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=-3 \\\\  & -{x}_{3}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265758 1072 #uuid "a5bd3a7f-0603-5a94-9bbf-0a46b897e249" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265758 1074 "The third equation states that \n\n$$\n-{x}_{3}=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{3}=1.\n$$\n\n Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+2(1)=-3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-3-2=-5.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=1$ and ${x}_{2}=-5$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}-(-5)+4(1)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{-1-5-4}{2}=-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is \n${x}_{1}=-5$, ${x}_{2}=-5$, and ${x}_{3}=1.$  In vector form, the solution can be written as $[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265759 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265661 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265759 1069 "q-129615" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265759 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265759 1071 "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-1 \\\\  & -{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=7 \\\\  & 2{x}_{3}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265759 1072 #uuid "86f65392-52cb-532d-9e01-57782fdc15f5" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265759 1074 "The third equation states that \n\n$$\n2{x}_{3}=4\\,⟹\\,{x}_{3}=2.\n$$\n\n Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-{x}_{2}+3(2)=7\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=6-7=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=-1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+5(-1)+(2)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-1+5-2=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is \n${x}_{1}=2$, ${x}_{2}=-1$, and ${x}_{3}=2.$  In vector form, the solution can be written as $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265760 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265580 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265760 1069 "q-129631" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265760 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265760 1071 "Suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(2,0)=0,\\,{f}_{x}(2,0)=0,\\,{f}_{y}(2,0)=2.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,0),$ estimate the value of $f(2.01,0.02).$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265760 1072 #uuid "dfb1cbc6-0efa-5400-ada3-fc9c8a1e12bb" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265760 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(2,0)+∇f(2,0)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-0⟩ \\\\  & =f(2,0)+⟨{f}_{x}(2,0),{f}_{y}(2,0)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y⟩ \\\\  & =0+⟨0,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(2.01,0.02)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=2.01$ and $y=0.02,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.01,0.02) & ≈⟨0,2⟩\\cdot ⟨2.01-2,0.02⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.02⟩ \\\\  & =0(0.01)+2(0.02) \\\\  & =0+0.04 \\\\  & =0.04\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265761 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265625 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265761 1069 "q-129632" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265761 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265761 1071 "Suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,-1)=-3,\\,{f}_{x}(1,-1)=-6,\\,{f}_{y}(1,-1)=3.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1),$ estimate the value of $f(1.01,-0.99).$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265761 1072 #uuid "c4947c49-4be9-5752-b327-3eab9b3c6101" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265761 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,-1)+∇f(1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,-1)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,-1),{f}_{y}(1,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩ \\\\  & =-3+⟨-6,3⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.01,-0.99)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.01$ and $y=-0.99,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.01,-0.99) & ≈-3+⟨-6,3⟩\\cdot ⟨1.01-1,-0.99+1⟩ \\\\  & =-3+⟨-6,3⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.01⟩ \\\\  & =-3-6(0.01)+3(0.01) \\\\  & =-3-0.06+0.03 \\\\  & =-3.03\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265762 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265461 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265762 1069 "q-129634" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265762 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265762 1071 "Suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,-1)=-2,\\,{f}_{x}(1,-1)=1,\\,{f}_{y}(1,-1)=6.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1),$ estimate the value of $f(0.96,-0.95).$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265762 1072 #uuid "71dfafbe-acbc-5c2b-a630-4510c3beefaf" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265762 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,-1)+∇f(1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,-1)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,-1),{f}_{y}(1,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩ \\\\  & =-2+⟨1,6⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(0.96,-0.95)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=0.96$ and $y=-0.95,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.96,-0.95) & ≈-2+⟨1,6⟩\\cdot ⟨0.96-1,-0.95+1⟩ \\\\  & =-2+⟨1,6⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.04,0.05⟩ \\\\  & =-2+1(-0.04)+6(0.05) \\\\  & =-2-0.04+0.3 \\\\  & =-1.74\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265763 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265671 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265763 1069 "q-129636" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265763 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265763 1071 "Let $f(x,y)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,3)=4,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y)=2x+y,\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)=x.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,3),$ estimate the value of $f(1.04,3.03).$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265763 1072 #uuid "46fcafee-3ead-50fb-9300-00e4d05cfb60" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265763 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,3)+∇f(1,3)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-3⟩ \\\\  & ≈f(1,3)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,3),{f}_{y}(1,3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-3⟩ \\\\  & ≈4+⟨{f}_{x}(1,3),{f}_{y}(1,3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(1,3)$ and ${f}_{y}(1,3)$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,3) & =2(1)+3=5 \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,3) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈4+⟨5,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-3⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(1.04,3.03)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.04$ and $y=3.03,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.04,3.03) & ≈4+⟨5,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1.04-1,3.03-3⟩ \\\\  & =4+⟨5,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0.04,0.03⟩ \\\\  & =4+5(0.04)+1(0.03) \\\\  & =4+0.2+0.03 \\\\  & =4.23\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265764 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265486 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265764 1069 "q-129637" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265764 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265764 1071 "Let $f(x,y)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,-1)=-2,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y)=2xy-{y}^{2},\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)={x}^{2}-2xy.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1),$ estimate the value of $f(0.95,-1.04).$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265764 1072 #uuid "a51164f2-25aa-5a0c-b9bd-2a60f94b38dd" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265764 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,-1)+∇f(1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & ≈f(1,-1)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,-1),{f}_{y}(1,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈-2+⟨{f}_{x}(1,-1),{f}_{y}(1,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(1,-1)$ and ${f}_{y}(1,-1)$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,-1) & =2(1)(-1)-(-1)^{2}=-3 \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,-1) & ={1}^{2}-2(1)(-1)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈-2+⟨-3,3⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(0.95,-1.04)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=0.95$ and $y=-1.04,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.95,-1.04) & ≈-2+⟨-3,3⟩\\cdot ⟨0.95-1,-1.04+1⟩ \\\\  & =-2+⟨-3,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.05,-0.04⟩ \\\\  & =-2-3(-0.05)+3(-0.04) \\\\  & =-2+0.15-0.12 \\\\  & =-1.97\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265765 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265646 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265765 1069 "q-129644" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265765 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265765 1071 "${\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265765 1072 #uuid "1bf34670-60be-5ee5-b116-ad2d01776177" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265765 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,{[xy+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[(2x+\\frac{8}{3})-(x+\\frac{1}{3})]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\frac{7}{3})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\frac{7}{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{7x}{3}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{7}{3})-(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{7}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265766 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265687 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265766 1069 "q-129645" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265766 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265766 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265766 1072 #uuid "69c9f9d1-65b3-5cdc-b505-3ad644b1c2be" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265766 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant. \n\nMaking a substitution $xy=u,$ $\\text{d}y=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}u,$ we get.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x[{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x[{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{x}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{e}^{u}]_{0}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{x}-1)\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{x}-1)\\,\\text{d}x & =[{e}^{x}-x]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(e-1)-(1-0) \\\\  & =e-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265767 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265415 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265767 1069 "q-12965" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265767 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265767 1071 "$(1+a)^{9}=$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265767 1072 #uuid "c1496f5e-966f-5bae-a9fd-d8a85554ae51" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265767 1074 "The first three terms of the binomial expansion for $(1+x)^{n}$ are given by\n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{n(n-1)}{2!}{x}^{2}+⋯.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $x=a$ and $n=9$ into the above formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+a)}^{9} & =1+9\\cdot a+\\frac{9(9-1)}{2!}\\cdot {a}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+9a+\\frac{9\\cdot 8}{2}\\cdot {a}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+9a+36{a}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265768 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265615 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265768 1069 "q-129654" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265768 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265768 1071 "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,0,-1)=1,\\,{f}_{x}(1,0,-1)=-1,\\,{f}_{y}(1,0,-1)=-1,\\,{f}_{z}(1,0,-1)=0.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,0,-1)$ estimate the value of $f(1.02,0.1,-0.96)$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265768 1072 #uuid "73178492-8508-56c4-9569-cdff37299fa0" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265768 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,0,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,0,-1)+∇f(1,0,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-0,z-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,0,-1)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,0,-1),{f}_{y}(1,0,-1),{f}_{z}(1,0,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y,z+1⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨-1,-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y,z+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.02,0.1,-0.96)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.02,$ $y=0.1,$ and $z=-0.96$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.02,0.1,-0.96) & ≈1+⟨-1,-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1.02-1,0.1,-0.96+1⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨-1,-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0.02,0.1,0.04⟩ \\\\  & =1-1(0.02)-1(0.1)+0(0.04) \\\\  & =1-0.02-0.1+0 \\\\  & =0.88\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265769 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265517 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265769 1069 "q-129655" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265769 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265769 1071 "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(-1,1,0)=-1,\\,{f}_{x}(-1,1,0)=1,\\,{f}_{y}(-1,1,0)=-2,\\,{f}_{z}(-1,1,0)=0.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,1,0)$ estimate the value of $f(-1,1.01,-0.01)$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265769 1072 #uuid "ed8e93d2-0997-5503-b2f5-fb92ac71a49d" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265769 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,1,0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(-1,1,0)+∇f(-1,1,0)\\cdot ⟨x-(-1),y-1,z-0⟩ \\\\  & =f(-1,1,0)+⟨{f}_{x}(-1,1,0),{f}_{y}(-1,1,0),{f}_{z}(-1,1,0)⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,y-1,z⟩ \\\\  & =-1+⟨1,-2,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,y-1,z⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-1,1.01,-0.01)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=-1,$ $y=1.01,$ and $z=-0.01$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1,1.01,-0.01) & ≈-1+⟨1,-2,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1+1,1.01-1,-0.01⟩ \\\\  & =-1+⟨1,-2,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0.01,-0.01⟩ \\\\  & =-1+1(0)-2(0.01)+0(-0.01) \\\\  & =-1+0-0.02+0 \\\\  & =-1.02\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265770 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265359 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265770 1069 "q-129657" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265770 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265770 1071 "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,2,3)=6,\\,{f}_{x}(1,2,3)=6,\\,{f}_{y}(1,2,3)=3,\\,{f}_{z}(1,2,3)=2.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,3)$ estimate the value of $f(1.08,1.93,3.05)$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265770 1072 #uuid "3f88dd94-3a57-5874-9049-b04644f1e878" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265770 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,2,3)+∇f(1,2,3)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-3⟩ \\\\  & =6+⟨{f}_{x}(1,2,3),{f}_{y}(1,2,3),{f}_{z}(1,2,3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-3⟩ \\\\  & =6+⟨6,3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.08,1.93,3.05)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.08,$ $y=1.93,$ and $z=3.05$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.08,1.93,3.05) & ≈6+⟨6,3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨1.08-1,1.93-2,3.05-3⟩ \\\\  & =6+⟨6,3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0.08,-0.07,0.05⟩ \\\\  & =6+6(0.08)+3(-0.07)+2(0.05) \\\\  & =6+0.48-0.21+0.1 \\\\  & =6.37\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265771 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265656 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265771 1069 "q-129658" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265771 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265771 1071 "![](images/b9/b946843e712c6294e261cb3015b02bc065cd265763805cf046009a2db0d11b83.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[-1,3]\\times [-2,1]$\n- $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n- $R=[-2,3]\\times [-2,2]$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265771 1072 #uuid "16d77304-e003-57b3-942e-c991453c4142" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265771 1074 "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and II do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle III covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265772 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265590 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265772 1069 "q-129661" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265772 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265772 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(0,1,-1)=-2,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y,z)=z{e}^{x},\\,{f}_{y}(x,y,z)=-1,\\,{f}_{z}(x,y,z)={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,1,-1),$ estimate the value of $f(0.01,1.01,-0.99).$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265772 1072 #uuid "7d09800e-3081-5040-b777-174413c7401e" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265772 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(0,1,-1)+∇f(0,1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-0,y-1,z-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & ≈-2+⟨{f}_{x}(0,1,-1),{f}_{y}(0,1,-1),{f}_{z}(0,1,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y-1,z+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(0,1,-1)$, ${f}_{y}(0,1,-1)$ and ${f}_{z}(0,1,-1),$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1,-1) & =(-1){e}^{0}=-1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,1,-1) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{z}(0,1,-1) & ={e}^{0}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈-2+⟨-1,-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y-1,z+1⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(0.01,1.01,-0.99)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=0.01,y=1.01$ and $y=-0.99,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.01,1.01,-0.99) & ≈-2+⟨-1,-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,1.01-1,-0.99+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈-2+⟨-1,-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.01,0.01⟩ \\\\  & ≈-2-1(0.01)-1(0.01)+1(0.01) \\\\  & ≈-2-0.01-0.01+0.01 \\\\  & ≈-2.01\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265773 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265353 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265773 1069 "q-129662" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265773 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265773 1071 "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}9-{x}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}(9-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[-3,3]\\times [-1,1]$\n- $R=[-4,4]\\times [-1,1]$\n- $R=[-3,3]\\times [0,2]$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265773 1072 #uuid "60b29014-e789-5b3b-ac4b-a01d242582dc" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265773 1074 "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/4a/4a8671db243a19fb258f6144d6aba1b193e237ef93d45d877829f80b93bc7d36.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}9-{x}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(9-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangle III does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangles I and II cover the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265774 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265311 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265774 1069 "q-129663" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265774 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265774 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(-1,0,1)=-1,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y,z)={z}^{2},\\,{f}_{y}(x,y,z)=1,\\,{f}_{z}(x,y,z)=2xz.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,0,1),$ estimate the value of $f(-0.95,0.02,1.04).$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265774 1072 #uuid "c2c83fb2-83c1-5c45-a264-53fc2a414e1c" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265774 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,0,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(-1,0,1)+∇f(-1,0,1)\\cdot ⟨x-(-1),y-0,z-1⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1+⟨{f}_{x}(-1,0,1),{f}_{y}(-1,0,1),{f}_{z}(-1,0,1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,y,z-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(-1,0,1)$, ${f}_{y}(-1,0,1)$ and ${f}_{z}(-1,0,1),$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-1,0,1) & ={1}^{2}=1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(-1,0,1) & =1 \\\\ {f}_{z}(-1,0,1) & =2(-1)(1)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈-1+⟨1,1,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,y,z-1⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(-0.95,0.02,1.04)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=-0.95,y=0.02$ and $y=1.04,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-0.95,0.02,1.04) & ≈-1+⟨1,1,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.95+1,0.02,1.04-1⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1+⟨1,1,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨0.05,0.02,0.04⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1+1(0.05)+1(0.02)-2(0.04) \\\\  & ≈-1+0.05+0.02-0.08 \\\\  & ≈-1.01\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265775 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265579 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265775 1069 "q-129666" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265775 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265775 1071 "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,1]$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265775 1072 #uuid "4a14dc47-818c-583a-957a-79ea6806459b" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265775 1074 "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/78/78af77d697ea292385e2bd2570c8185c728f57cc70c3edcc25ecccd0e84780e8.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles II and III do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle I cover the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265776 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265458 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265776 1069 "q-129744" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265776 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265776 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,\\pi ).$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265776 1072 #uuid "9e5cd9f0-13d2-5033-ac0f-ba439af98407" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265776 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,\\pi )$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-1,\\pi )+∇f(-1,\\pi )\\cdot ⟨x+1,y-\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(-1,\\pi )=1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(-1,\\pi )=-1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(-1,\\pi )=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(-1,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-1,\\pi ) & =⟨-1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-1,\\pi )+∇f(-1,\\pi )\\cdot ⟨x+1,y-\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & ≈1+⟨-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,y-\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & ≈1-(x+1)+0 \\\\  & ≈-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265777 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265373 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265777 1069 "q-129745" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265777 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265777 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-y.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,-1).$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265777 1072 #uuid "3615abea-583e-5e5f-b0c4-561023b77ca5" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265777 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(0,-1)+∇f(0,-1)\\cdot ⟨x,y+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(0,-1)=1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =4x\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(0,-1)=0, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =-1 &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(0,-1)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(0,-1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(0,-1) & =⟨0,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(0,-1)+∇f(0,-1)\\cdot ⟨x,y+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈1+⟨0,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈1+0-(y+1) \\\\  & ≈-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265778 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265267 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265778 1069 "q-129746" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265778 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265778 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)=yz-x.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,-1,1).$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265778 1072 #uuid "7f8175f3-53d3-588b-a7a3-5b2abc348ee7" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265778 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,-1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(0,-1,1)+∇f(0,-1,1)\\cdot ⟨x,y+1,z-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(0,-1,1)=-1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =-1\\,⇒\\,{f}_{x}(0,-1,1)=-1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =z\\,⇒\\,{f}_{y}(0,-1,1)=1, \\\\ {f}_{z} & =y\\,⇒\\,{f}_{z}(0,-1,1)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(0,-1,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(0,-1,1) & =⟨{f}_{x}(0,-1,1),{f}_{y}(0,-1,1),{f}_{z}(0,-1,1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(0,-1,1)+∇f(0,-1,1)\\cdot ⟨x,y+1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1+⟨-1,1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y+1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1-(x)+(y+1)-(z-1) \\\\  & ≈1-x+y-z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265779 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265395 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265779 1069 "q-129748" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265779 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265779 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1,-1).$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265779 1072 #uuid "5440338c-d1fc-538a-8b78-57b3659475c9" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265779 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,-1,-1)+∇f(1,-1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(1,-1,-1)=1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =yz\\,⇒\\,{f}_{x}(1,-1,-1)=1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =xz\\,⇒\\,{f}_{y}(1,-1,-1)=-1, \\\\ {f}_{z} & =xy\\,⇒\\,{f}_{z}(1,-1,-1)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(1,-1,-1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(1,-1,-1) & =⟨{f}_{x}(1,-1,-1),{f}_{y}(1,-1,-1),{f}_{z}(1,-1,-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,-1,-1)+∇f(1,-1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈1+⟨1,-1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈1+(x-1)-(y+1)-(z+1) \\\\  & ≈-2+x-y-z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265780 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265588 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265780 1069 "q-129783" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265780 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265780 1071 "![](images/77/779f39d9b8eca56cf8b7b2b9355364cd95171ca61c3e07cabd69e7a048933e22.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [-2,3]$\n- ${∬}_{R}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-3,3]$\n- ${∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265780 1072 #uuid "9794411d-a692-56fa-886b-52950afee48e" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265780 1074 "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement III is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265781 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265700 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265781 1069 "q-129785" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265781 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265781 1071 "![](images/d3/d3b5a9d779cddfb94e6bffc73c273dcc6df997259e09aed0c1b2db7c9529bc4e.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∬}_{D}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,3]\\times [-2,2]$\n- ${∬}_{R}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,4]\\times [-3,3]$\n- ${∬}_{D}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,4]\\times [-3,3]$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265781 1072 #uuid "827c070f-3c9e-5786-8e70-6f28feb2b294" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265781 1074 "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement III is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265782 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265325 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265782 1069 "q-12979" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265782 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265782 1071 "The equation ${x}^{2}+4x-7=0$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^{2}=q.$   Find $p+q.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265782 1072 #uuid "e7c6376f-3050-50c0-ac00-dc867cf23b29" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265782 1074 "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x-7 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x & =7 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2\\cdot x & =7 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2\\cdot x+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2} & =7 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}-4 & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}-4 & =7 \\\\ (x+2)^{2} & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $p=2,$ $q=11,$ and $p+q=13.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265783 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265350 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265783 1069 "q-129797" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265783 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265783 1071 "Compute the Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ for ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265783 1072 #uuid "59cfc6f0-d08d-5f61-ba3e-7216ac027397" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265783 1074 "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =(x){(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{′}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x){(x)}^{′} \\\\  & =(x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(1) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265784 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265658 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265784 1069 "q-12980" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265784 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265784 1071 "The equation ${a}^{2}+8a=18$ can be written in the form $(a+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation." 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265784 1072 #uuid "3e3748c5-6be5-535f-b3c7-81c495d312b4" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265784 1074 "First, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+8a & =18 \\\\ {a}^{2}+2\\cdot 4\\cdot a & =18 \\\\ {a}^{2}+2\\cdot a\\cdot 4+{4}^{2}-{4}^{2} & =18 \\\\ (a+4)^{2}-{4}^{2} & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a+4)^{2}-{4}^{2} & =18 \\\\ (a+4)^{2}-16 & =18 \\\\ (a+4)^{2} & =34\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=4,q=34,$ and $p+q=38.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a+4)^{2} & =34 \\\\ \\sqrt{(a+4)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{34} \\\\ |a+4| & =\\sqrt{34} \\\\ a+4 & =\\pm \\sqrt{34} \\\\ a & =-4\\pm \\sqrt{34}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265785 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265662 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265785 1069 "q-129800" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265785 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265785 1071 "Compute the Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ for ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265785 1072 #uuid "9ce6b8cc-3f3b-55bd-a0b8-d4825ae956b1" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265785 1074 "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =(x){(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{′}-(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x){(x)}^{′} \\\\  & =x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(1) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265576 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265786 1069 "q-12983" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265786 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265786 1071 "The equation $4{z}^{2}+4z=12$ can be written in the form $(z+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation." 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265786 1072 #uuid "e3fdc752-039f-5f05-8441-586cbafa095b" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265786 1074 "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, divide it by the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{z}^{2}+4z & =12 \\\\ 4({z}^{2}+z) & =12 \\\\ {z}^{2}+z & =3 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot z & =3 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot z+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{12}{4} \\\\ {(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{13}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=\\frac{1}{2},q=\\frac{13}{4},$ and $p+q=\\frac{15}{4}.$ \n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{13}{4}} \\\\ |z+\\frac{1}{2}| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2} \\\\ z+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2} \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2} \\\\ z & =\\frac{-1\\pm \\sqrt{13}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265787 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265659 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265787 1069 "q-129836" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265787 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265787 1071 "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-2y=0$ where $y=y(x).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265787 1072 #uuid "44bbf18b-7ba9-5ecf-acfa-bc2974557813" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265787 1074 "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-2{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -2)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-1$ or $\\lambda =2.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265788 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265703 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265788 1069 "q-129898" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265788 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265788 1071 "![](images/c3/c319243db36af6829d83c8d5dfd7a724d884ff2506917249d324a315fd77ac28.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,3],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in D\\cap R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265788 1072 #uuid "e6fd11b1-4185-5d3c-b727-63499b4e3d6c" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265788 1074 "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/e7/e79856c97ab7e4dd27bb1f476fd911c2e7d77aac6ab54fe50597b3aafbca720c.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function I. This function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Functions II and III are equals $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265789 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265277 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265789 1069 "q-129904" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265789 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265789 1071 "![](images/f8/f8cb975b622bfe420d9418587293e83de60c297019f15e1b379bc118e079d35d.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265789 1072 #uuid "d2768639-7882-5bba-9579-987ae66a0169" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265789 1074 "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/89/8944afb608465e49c7ae253f8e2518aa3d43b1c6e121dd91178ca1a67116a508.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions I and III. Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function II equals $3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265790 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265593 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265790 1069 "q-129906" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265790 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265790 1071 "![](images/1b/1b1663fc5459119be23806dece661979722ac2ef507add83f3e71d7799246130.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-3,3]\\times [-2,3],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ x+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265790 1072 #uuid "36b8ecd0-d692-515d-8583-af8deca52b58" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265790 1074 "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/bc/bc6d7409dee2b5935d03839d32fc3aad23f08f1dd2c65eae496df8f995960b23.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $x+{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function I. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,3)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\n- Functions II and III are equals to $x+{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265791 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265681 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265791 1069 "q-129909" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265791 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265791 1071 "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-2$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+1.$ If $R=[-3,2]\\times [-2,3],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{2}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265791 1072 #uuid "ef54449c-2307-5871-89d3-1b2194748b24" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265791 1074 "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/41/414a5c8c14d70ff08d607c2a755f565c147b8b5e691a8d38a9e7db5697ba9e48.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal ${2}^{x+y}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{2}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function I. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(0,3)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\n- Functions II and III equal ${2}^{x+y}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265792 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265429 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265792 1069 "q-129936" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265792 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265792 1071 "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}\\frac{{y}^{2}+1}{x}\\,\\text{d}A$ over the rectangle $R=[1,e]\\times [0,3].$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265792 1072 #uuid "9df605e6-74d2-5d70-8d46-67b7ab55e508" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265792 1074 "We apply the multiplicative property of double integrals and then evaluate each of the integrals separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{{y}^{2}+1}{x}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}(\\frac{1}{x})({y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =({\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{3}({y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]_{1}^{e}\\cdot [\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+y]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\cdot (\\frac{{3}^{3}}{3}+3) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265793 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265397 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265793 1069 "q-129937" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265793 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265793 1071 "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$ over the rectangle $R=[0,\\pi ]\\times [0,2].$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265793 1072 #uuid "78952ad2-30a2-59fb-b1fc-a12708c43533" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265793 1074 "We apply the multiplicative property of double integrals and then evaluate each of the integrals separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(\\frac{1}{4+{y}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]_{0}^{\\pi }\\cdot [\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-(-1-1)\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{8}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265794 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265583 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265794 1069 "q-129942" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265794 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265794 1071 "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265794 1072 #uuid "edcd3710-0bbf-55f6-957d-428926b6e84b" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265794 1074 "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{x}+2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{x}+2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x})-2({u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x})+u{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{x}+2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x}-2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}-2u{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{x}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=({c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}){e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={e}^{2x}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}.\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265795 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265551 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265795 1069 "q-12995" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265795 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265795 1071 "The equation ${z}^{2}-25=-10z$ can be written in the form $(z+p{)}^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265795 1072 #uuid "b2094d9e-dc94-52c2-adb6-43e9a1505bb1" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265795 1074 "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, the $-10z$ term to the left-hand side, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}-25 & =-10z \\\\ {z}^{2}+10z & =25 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot z & =25 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot z+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =25 \\\\ (z+5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =25 \\\\ (z+5{)}^{2} & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=5,q=50,$ and $p+q=55.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265796 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265471 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265796 1069 "q-129956" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265796 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265796 1071 "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{-2t}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265796 1072 #uuid "a1408d84-fcf4-5ea6-946a-9cd3b9087562" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265796 1074 "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){e}^{-2t}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}-2u{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{-2t}-4{u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}+4u{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{-2t}-4{u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}+4u{e}^{-2t})+5({u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}-2u{e}^{-2t})+6u{e}^{-2t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{-2t}-4{u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}+4u{e}^{-2t}+5{u}^{′}{e}^{-2t}-10u{e}^{-2t}+6u{e}^{-2t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{-2t}+{u}^{′}{e}^{-2t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-2t}({u}^{″}+{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-2t}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}+{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}+w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}\\,⟹\\,u=-{c}_{1}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{e}^{-2t}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=(-{c}_{1}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}){e}^{-2t}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-t}\\cdot {e}^{-2t}={e}^{-3t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-{e}^{-5t}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-3t}.\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265797 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265507 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265797 1069 "q-129957" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265797 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265797 1071 "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{4t}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-16y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265797 1072 #uuid "07185561-6548-5175-bde3-1e35630955ed" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265797 1074 "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){e}^{4t}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{4t}+4u{e}^{4t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{4t}+8{u}^{′}{e}^{4t}+16u{e}^{4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{4t}+8{u}^{′}{e}^{4t}+16u{e}^{4t})-16u{e}^{4t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{4t}+8{u}^{′}{e}^{4t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{4t}({u}^{″}+8{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{4t}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}+8{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}+8w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{e}^{-8t}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{e}^{-8t}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{8}{e}^{-8t}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{e}^{4t}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{8}{e}^{-8t}+{c}_{2}){e}^{4t}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-8$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-8t}\\cdot {e}^{4t}={e}^{-4t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-8\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4t}+B{e}^{-4t}.\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265798 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265499 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265798 1069 "q-129961" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265798 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265798 1071 "If\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nthen $AB=$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265798 1072 #uuid "c109d94f-badb-560f-816a-dc72e0193e2a" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265798 1074 "Here, $A$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix and $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in $A$ and $1$ row in $B,$ so $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication: \n\n$$\n{A}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 2}={AB}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $AB,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}], &  & \\,-3=-1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}], &  & \\,1=(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & ∗\\end{aligned}], &  & \\,-9=(-3)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 3\\end{aligned}], &  & \\,3=(-3)\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$AB=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 3\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265799 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265688 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265799 1069 "q-129971" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265799 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265799 1071 "Given that $X∼B(4,\\frac{1}{3}),$ compute $P(X<3).$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265799 1072 #uuid "68512d61-c123-5b4f-bd6e-2abe384cfbfb" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265799 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(4,\\frac{1}{3}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{0}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{0}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4-2} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{0}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}+4{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}+6{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265800 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265433 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265800 1069 "q-129973" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265800 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265800 1071 "Given $X∼B(5,\\frac{1}{3}),$ compute $P(1\\le X\\le 2).$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265800 1072 #uuid "e0316062-8105-57c8-93fe-257f0fc7faf0" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265800 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(5,\\frac{1}{3}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in {1,2}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{5-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{5-2} \\\\  & =5{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}+10{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{160}{243}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265801 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265558 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265801 1069 "q-130003" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265801 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265801 1071 "Consider the differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ is a solution to the equation\n- ${y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution to the equation\n- The general solution of the equation is $y=Ax+Bx\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265801 1072 #uuid "a94ff3b3-b6c6-5496-9eb9-eaf4ceef3036" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265801 1074 "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =1 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(0)-x(1)+x=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(\\frac{1}{x})-x(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- So, both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian.\n\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =(x)(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}-(x)^{′}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =x(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-(1)x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not equal to $0,$ the functions are linearly independent, hence $y=Ax+Bx\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$  is the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is true." 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265802 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265548 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265802 1069 "q-130006" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265802 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265802 1071 "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}$ on $A?$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265802 1072 #uuid "1b5f24a7-25be-5f91-9261-490e6919b435" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265802 1074 "The operation ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}$ means that we need to multiply the third row of the matrix by $\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nMultiplying the $3$rd row by $\\frac{1}{3}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 6 & \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9 & \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265803 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265408 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265803 1069 "q-130012" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265803 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265803 1071 "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)=\\frac{1}{t}$ is a solution to the equation $2{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+3t{y}^{′}-y=0$ for $t>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265803 1072 #uuid "2b4ddb18-0f3e-54a1-992c-70260602e2de" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265803 1074 "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){t}^{-1}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{t}^{-1}-u{t}^{-2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{t}^{-1}-2{u}^{′}{t}^{-2}+2u{t}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{-1}-2{u}^{′}{t}^{-2}+2u{t}^{-3})+3t({u}^{′}{t}^{-1}-u{t}^{-2})-u{t}^{-1} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}t-4{u}^{′}+4u{t}^{-1}+3{u}^{′}-3u{t}^{-1}-u{t}^{-1} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}t-{u}^{′} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n2t{w}^{′}-w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}\\sqrt{t}\\,⟹\\,u=\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{3}{t}^{3/2}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{t}^{-1}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=(\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{3}{t}^{3/2}+{c}_{2}){t}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{3/2}\\cdot {t}^{-1}={t}^{1/2}=\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{A}{t}+B\\sqrt{t}.\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265804 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265298 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265804 1069 "q-130017" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265804 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265804 1071 "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={t}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}-4t{y}^{′}+6y=0$ for $t>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265804 1072 #uuid "31386a54-f6f9-57eb-a75f-ecb8a5743f83" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265804 1074 "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){t}^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{′}t+2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{′}t+2u)-4t({u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut)+6u{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{4}+4{u}^{′}{t}^{3}+2u{t}^{2}-4{u}^{′}{t}^{3}-8u{t}^{2}+6u{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{4} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nThe solution is\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{t}^{2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=({c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}){t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=t\\cdot {t}^{2}={t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={t}^{4}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{t}^{2}+B{t}^{3}.\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265805 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265604 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265805 1069 "q-130020" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265805 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265805 1071 "Given that $C$ and $D$ are finite, non-overlapping regions and that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{C}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=12,\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=-8,\n$$\n\nfind the value of \n\n$$\n{∬}_{C\\cup D}3f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A.\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265805 1072 #uuid "f115877f-b6af-5109-a7e3-50664e948f11" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265805 1074 "Using the linearity and additivity of double integrals, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{C\\cup D}3f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =3{∬}_{C\\cup D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3({∬}_{C}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A) \\\\  & =3(12-8) \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265806 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265571 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265806 1069 "q-130028" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265806 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265806 1071 "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)=t$ is a solution to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}+2t{y}^{′}-2y=0$ for $t>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265806 1072 #uuid "fa1dae64-83b4-59f1-8ce4-3c9cac8a6ed9" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265806 1074 "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t)t.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}t+u \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}t+2{u}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}t+2{u}^{′})+2t({u}^{′}t+u)-2ut & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{3}+2{u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2{u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut-2ut & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{3}+4{u}^{′}{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}({u}^{″}t+4{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}t+4{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}t+4w=0.\n$$\n\nThe solution is\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{4}}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3{t}^{3}}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=ut$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3{t}^{3}}+{c}_{2})t.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-3$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}\\cdot t=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=At+\\frac{B}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265807 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265614 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265807 1069 "q-130029" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265807 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265807 1071 "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{3}{4}),$ compute $P(X=3).$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265807 1072 #uuid "5e85e1e8-1d06-5f95-8298-450b9ef27fd8" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265807 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{3}{4}),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x-1}(\\frac{3}{4}), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =f(3) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265808 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265601 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265808 1069 "q-130030" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265808 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265808 1071 "$|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|=$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265808 1072 #uuid "81986ab9-3781-515c-bf87-0ba201cbe866" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265808 1074 "The $4$th row of the matrix contains two zeros, more than any other row. So using a Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{41}+(-2)\\cdot {C}_{42}+1\\cdot {C}_{43}+0\\cdot {C}_{44} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)^{4+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot (-1)^{4+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265809 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265561 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265809 1069 "q-130031" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265809 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265809 1071 "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.4),$ compute $P(X=5).$  Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265809 1072 #uuid "e412f016-1827-5a09-955d-1fa10d7d3776" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265809 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.4),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{(0.6)}^{x-1}(0.4), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & ={(0.6)}^{4}(0.4) \\\\  & ≈0.0518.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265810 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265492 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265810 1069 "q-130038" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265810 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265810 1071 "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+1}{1+{x}^{2}},\\, & \\text{if}\\,1<x<2,\\,1<y<2, \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}},\\, & \\text{if}\\,1<x<2,\\,2<y<4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n{∬}_{(1,2)\\times (1,4)}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265810 1072 #uuid "4097ab0e-1da6-536a-8ecd-ac95516f4e76" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265810 1074 "First, notice that the rectangle $R$ can be represented as a union of two smaller rectangles, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =(1,2)\\times ((1,2)\\cup (2,4)) \\\\  & =((1,2)\\times (1,2))\\cup ((1,2)\\times (2,4)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{(1,2)\\times (1,2)}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{(1,2)\\times (2,4)}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{(1,2)\\times (1,2)}\\frac{y+1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{(1,2)\\times (2,4)}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265811 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265504 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265811 1069 "q-130039" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265811 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265811 1071 "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<\\pi ,\\,0<y<\\frac{\\pi }{4}, \\\\ \\sqrt{x}{sec}^{2}⁡y,\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<\\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}<y<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n{∬}_{(0,\\pi )\\times (0,\\pi /2)}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265811 1072 #uuid "f51278fd-72a7-5676-a049-adc6f403da6d" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265811 1074 "First, notice that the rectangle $R$ can be represented as a union of two smaller rectangles, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =(0,\\pi )\\times ((0,\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =((0,\\pi )\\times (0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}))\\cup ((0,\\pi )\\times (\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{(0,\\pi )\\times (0,\\pi /4)}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{(0,\\pi )\\times (\\pi /4,\\pi /2)}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{(0,\\pi )\\times (0,\\pi /4)}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{(0,\\pi )\\times (\\pi /4,\\pi /2)}\\sqrt{x}{sec}^{2}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265812 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265483 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265812 1069 "q-130040" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265812 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265812 1071 "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation $2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-15y=0$ for $x>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265812 1072 #uuid "ce8f178b-217a-592c-86ce-c1cebc15d615" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265812 1074 "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux)+x({u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2})-15u{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}{x}^{5}+12{u}^{′}{x}^{4}+12u{x}^{3}+{u}^{′}{x}^{4}+3u{x}^{3}-15u{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}{x}^{5}+13{u}^{′}{x}^{4} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}(2{u}^{″}x+13{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n2{u}^{″}x+13{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n2{w}^{′}x+13w=0.\n$$\n\nThe solution is\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{x}^{-13/2}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{x}^{-13/2}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{11}{x}^{-11/2}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{3}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=(-\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{11}{x}^{-11/2}+{c}_{2}){x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-\\frac{11}{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{-11/2}\\cdot {x}^{3}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{5/2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-\\frac{11}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{5/2}}.\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265813 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265463 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265813 1069 "q-130042" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265813 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265813 1071 "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$, where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{2},\\, & \\text{if}\\,-2<x<0,\\,0<y<2, \\\\ 2x,\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,0<y<2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265813 1072 #uuid "a400e754-c19b-557c-be08-7c8b69cf6b10" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265813 1074 "First, notice that we have $R={R}_{1}\\cup {R}_{2}$, where ${R}_{1}=(-2,0)\\times (0,2)$ and ${R}_{2}=(0,1)\\times (0,2).$ So, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A= & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}2x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}(x)({y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}2x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =({\\int }_{-2}^{0}x\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y)+(2-0){\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{-2}^{0}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2}+2[{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\frac{8}{3}+2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265814 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265391 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265814 1069 "q-130044" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265814 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265814 1071 "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$, where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{x+1},\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,-2<y<0, \\\\ 3y\\sqrt{x},\\, & \\text{if}\\,1<x<4,\\,-2<y<0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265814 1072 #uuid "a87d3263-74f6-5090-88d6-56cb978e87ed" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265814 1074 "First, notice that we have $R={R}_{1}\\cup {R}_{2}$, where ${R}_{1}=(0,1)\\times (-2,0)$ and ${R}_{2}=(1,4)\\times (-2,0).$ So, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A= & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}y{e}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}3y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}({e}^{x+1})(y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}(\\sqrt{x})(3y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =({\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{-2}^{0}y\\,\\text{d}y)+({\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{-2}^{0}3y\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =[{e}^{x+1}]_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{-2}^{0}+{[\\frac{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{3}]}_{1}^{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =({e}^{2}-e)\\cdot (0-2)+(\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot (0-6) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2}+2e-28.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265815 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265367 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265815 1069 "q-130064" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265815 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265815 1071 "Consider the matrix $C,$ given by\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the following row operations on $C?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265815 1072 #uuid "89540d9f-9bfa-5f07-91be-e72783b1a7bc" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265815 1074 "Let's list the required operations:\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2}$ means that we need to add $-2$ times the second row to the first row.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}$ means that we need to add $1$ times the second row to the third row.\n\nApplying the operations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0+(-2)(5) & 4+(-2)(2) & 7+(-2)(1) \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(1)\\cdot {R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1+(1)(5) & 3+(1)(2) & 0+(1)(1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265816 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265502 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265816 1069 "q-130065" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265816 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265816 1071 "Consider the matrix $C,$ given by\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the following row operations on $C?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+3{R}_{3} \\\\ {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265816 1072 #uuid "873c8162-c75c-5ddc-aed6-146d6160fb0b" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265816 1074 "Let's list the required operations:\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{3}$ means that we need to add $3$ times the third row to the first row.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{3}$ means that we need to add $5$ times the third row to the second row.\n\nApplying the operations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1+(3)(2) & 2+(3)(0) & 1+(3)(-1) \\\\ -9 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -9 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -9+(5)(2) & 0+(5)(0) & -4+(5)(-1) \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265817 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265309 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265817 1069 "q-130070" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265817 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265817 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 5 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -9 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ on $A?$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265817 1072 #uuid "926d4b45-89b4-5d26-9733-b0d97c69c160" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265817 1074 "The operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ means that we need to swap the first and second rows.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 5 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -9 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 5 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 9 \\\\ -1 & -9 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265818 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265402 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265818 1069 "q-130072" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265818 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265818 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $V,$ given by\n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 9 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 6 & 7 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 5 & 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the following row operations on $V?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\ {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265818 1072 #uuid "25230851-35bf-5052-9d49-2045d4671518" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265818 1074 "Let's list the required operations:\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}$ means that we need to swap the first and third rows.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{3}$ means that we need to add $-1$ times the third row to the second row.\n\nApplying the operations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 9 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 6 & 7 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 5 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 6 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 9 & -2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 4+(-1)(-1) & 4+(-1)(9) & 6+(-1)(-2) & 7+(-1)(6) \\\\ -1 & 9 & -2 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 8 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 9 & -2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265819 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265324 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265819 1069 "q-130076" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265819 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265819 1071 "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})={x}^{2}-3x$ and $L({y}_{2})=x(x+1)$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L({y}_{1}+3{y}_{2})?$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265819 1072 #uuid "cf3a8d57-aa56-54fe-acf7-669db2497e9a" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265819 1074 "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L({y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}) & =L({y}_{1})+3L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3x+3\\cdot x(x+1) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3x+3{x}^{2}+3x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+3{x}^{2})+(3x-3x) \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}+0 \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265820 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265432 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265820 1069 "q-130077" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265820 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265820 1071 "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})={e}^{x}+1$ and $L({y}_{2})=2{e}^{x}-3$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2})?$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265820 1072 #uuid "9649d92d-19bf-5779-b0f0-45606506623f" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265820 1074 "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & =2L({y}_{1})-L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =2({e}^{x}+1)-(2{e}^{x}-3) \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}+2-2{e}^{x}+3 \\\\  & =(2{e}^{x}-2{e}^{x})+(2+3) \\\\  & =0+5 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265821 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265349 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265821 1069 "q-130079" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265821 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265821 1071 "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})={cot}^{2}⁡x$ and $L({y}_{2})=3{csc}^{2}⁡x-4$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L(4{y}_{1}-{y}_{2})?$\n\n*Hint: Use ${csc}^{2}⁡\\theta =1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta .$*" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265821 1072 #uuid "82cdef1c-33a0-5842-9242-53d73aca99a4" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265821 1074 "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(4{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & =4L({y}_{1})-L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {cot}^{2}⁡x-(3{csc}^{2}⁡x-4) \\\\  & =4{cot}^{2}⁡x+4-3{csc}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =4(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)-3{csc}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =4{csc}^{2}⁡x-3{csc}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & ={csc}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265822 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265655 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265822 1069 "q-130080" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265822 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265822 1071 "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})=2-{sin}^{2}⁡x$ and $L({y}_{2})=3{cos}^{2}⁡x$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L(3{y}_{1}-{y}_{2})?$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265822 1072 #uuid "bfd36b47-f530-50b4-b4b7-119c298890d2" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265822 1074 "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(3{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & =3L({y}_{1})-L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (2-{sin}^{2}⁡x)-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =6-3{sin}^{2}⁡x-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =6-3({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =6-3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265823 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265702 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265823 1069 "q-130081" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265823 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265823 1071 "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})=3x-5$ and $L({y}_{2})=(x-3)^{2}$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L(2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2})?$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265823 1072 #uuid "90434f36-e6cb-59b9-8b48-0ed0424bb2ec" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265823 1074 "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) & =2L({y}_{1})+L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (3x-5)+(x-3)^{2} \\\\  & =6x-10+{x}^{2}-6x+9 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+(6x-6x)+(-10+9) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265824 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265452 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265824 1069 "q-130082" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265824 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265824 1071 "Given that $D=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},$ what is $D(y)$ for $y={x}^{2}-2x?$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265824 1072 #uuid "9609d17b-a80b-5770-9742-b3ac0be699a9" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265824 1074 "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=x,{f}_{1}=1,$ and ${f}_{0}=0.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D({x}^{2}-2x) \\\\  & =(x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x})({x}^{2}-2x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({x}^{2}-2x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-2x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-2)+(2x-2) \\\\  & =2x+2x-2 \\\\  & =4x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265825 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265380 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265825 1069 "q-130083" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265825 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265825 1071 "Given that $D=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},$ what is $D(y)$ for $y={e}^{-2x}+x?$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265825 1072 #uuid "196a3908-7caf-585e-844a-b9f034733d8d" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265825 1074 "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={a}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{a}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{a}_{0}y\n$$\n\nwith constant coefficients  ${a}_{2}=1,{a}_{1}=2,$ and ${a}_{0}=0.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D({e}^{-2x}+x) \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x})({e}^{-2x}+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({e}^{-2x}+x)+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{-2x}+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{e}^{-2x}+1)+2(-2{e}^{-2x}+1) \\\\  & =4{e}^{-2x}-4{e}^{-2x}+2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265826 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265300 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265826 1069 "q-130087" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265826 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265826 1071 "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $A$ into the matrix $B$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 10 & 5 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265826 1072 #uuid "b8c55c12-17df-581d-b93b-78d07db8cec0" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265826 1074 "First, notice that the $1$st and the $2$nd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has changed is the $3$rd row. \n\nBy considering the given row operations that modify the $3$rd row, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 10 & 5 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {R}_{3}.\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265827 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265399 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265827 1069 "q-130092" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265827 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265827 1071 "$|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -7 & 2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}|=$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265827 1072 #uuid "ba53d6e2-edac-5757-8fc8-0a9f2f226683" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265827 1074 "Using a cofactor expansion along the $1$st column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -7 & 2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+-7\\cdot {C}_{21}+0\\cdot {C}_{31}+0\\cdot {C}_{41} \\\\  & =(-7)\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265828 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265490 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265828 1069 "q-130095" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265828 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265828 1071 "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix  $C$ into the matrix $D$, where \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265828 1072 #uuid "84b806b0-6ebe-59b8-9bb2-7c89ea346a6f" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265828 1074 "First, notice that the $2$nd and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has changed is the $1$st row. \n\nBy considering the given row operations that modify the $1$st row, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=3\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=3\\cdot {R}_{1}.\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265829 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265563 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265829 1069 "q-130096" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265829 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265829 1071 "Given the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1),$ find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-1,0).$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265829 1072 #uuid "54ba14c7-96f4-538f-92f9-9aee3ad220a5" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265829 1074 "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,0) & =\\frac{2(-1)}{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2(0)}{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+1}\\,j \\\\  & =(-1)i+0\\,j \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(-1,0)\\cdot ⟨x-(-1),\\,y-0⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265830 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265626 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265830 1069 "q-130100" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265830 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265830 1071 "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $E$ into the matrix $F$, where\n\n$$\nE=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,F=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265830 1072 #uuid "85e6dfb7-2447-51c4-a184-4a363a6b54d0" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265830 1074 "First, notice that the $1$st row is the same in both matrices. The only rows that have changed are the $2$nd row and the $3$rd row.   \n\nBy considering the given row operations that change both the $2$nd and the $3$rd row, we notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =F.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}.\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265831 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265690 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265831 1069 "q-130103" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265831 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265831 1071 "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $X$ into the matrix $W$, where\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 10 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -5 & -10 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 10 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265831 1072 #uuid "8d79bcd3-c9f6-51cc-bac3-1326fcc4218c" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265831 1074 "First, notice that the $2$nd and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has changed is the $1$st row. \n\nBy considering the given row operations that modify the $1$st row, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 10 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+5\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & -5 & -10 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 10 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =W.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+5{R}_{3}.\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265832 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265567 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265832 1069 "q-130104" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265832 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265832 1071 "Given the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-4xy+5{y}^{2}$ find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-1,-1).$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265832 1072 #uuid "a7c798f0-d19a-5ddc-9430-afe4c5122532" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265832 1074 "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-4xy+5{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-4xy+5{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2x-4y)\\,i+(10y-4x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,-1) & =(2(-1)-4(-1))\\,i+(10(-1)-4(-1))\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i-6\\,j \\\\  & =⟨2,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(-1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-(-1),\\,y-(-1)⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨2,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y+1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265833 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265550 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265833 1069 "q-130107" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265833 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265833 1071 "Given that $E=5I$, where $I$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix, what is the value of $det(E)?$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265833 1072 #uuid "f02c11bc-7be8-588a-87f7-4b88513b473d" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265833 1074 "First, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5I & =5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a diagonal matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}| & =5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5=125.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265834 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265462 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265834 1069 "q-130111" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265834 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265834 1071 "Let $S=UV.$ What is the value of ${s}_{13}$, if\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 10 & 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265834 1072 #uuid "945d4e42-e131-5958-8aed-5486accd9a01" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265834 1074 "Multiplying the $1$st row in $U$ by the $3$rd column in $V$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{13} & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 10 & 2 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 3+3\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-6-6+5 \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265835 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265427 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265835 1069 "q-130114" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265835 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265835 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y=0.\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265835 1072 #uuid "480d405e-b6dc-5f02-bd47-92890e02436f" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265835 1074 "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-2{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -1)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =1.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{x}.\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265836 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265265 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265836 1069 "q-130118" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265836 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265836 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=0.\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265836 1072 #uuid "aabd9305-103b-5e3f-a7eb-59e7abf9e597" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265836 1074 "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-5\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+4{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +4) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +4=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -1)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =1$ or $\\lambda =4.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{4x}.\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265837 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265512 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265837 1069 "q-130120" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265837 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265837 1071 "Let $D=AB.$ What is the value of ${d}_{24}$, if\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -7\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265837 1072 #uuid "131c2176-2405-5c54-b473-9fb3958cf472" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265837 1074 "Multiplying the $2$nd row in $A$ by the $4$th column in $B$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{24} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-3)+4\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-9+4+3 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186265838 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265326 13194139533400 175