ned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.2)=0.4207\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.4207)=-0.2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-1-\\mu }{5} & =-0.2 \\\\ -1-\\mu  & =-1 \\\\ \\mu  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266151 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265526 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266151 1069 "q-132146" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266151 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266151 1071 "Calculate the value of \n$\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 1 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}|.$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{33}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266151 1072 #uuid "828f8227-98b6-5883-87ff-011f4c34ac7e" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266151 1074 "Using elementary column operations, let's make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{33}=2$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 1 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+(-2){C}_{3} \\\\ {C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+2{C}_{3} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $3$rd row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-5)\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =10(25+9) \\\\  & =340.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266152 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265294 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266152 1069 "q-132147" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266152 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266152 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(\\mu ,{2}^{2})$ with $P(X>2)=0.3085,$ find $\\mu .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6915\\,$ | $\\,0.6950\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ |" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266152 1072 #uuid "90f1401b-4bfd-570a-9950-c8cc06975066" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266152 1074 "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =1-P(X>2) \\\\  & =1-0.3085 \\\\  & =0.6915\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =0.6915 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{2-\\mu }{2}) & =0.6915 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{2-\\mu }{2}) & =0.6915 \\\\ \\frac{2-\\mu }{2} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.6915)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.5)=0.6915\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.6915)=0.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-\\mu }{2} & =0.5 \\\\ 2-\\mu  & =1 \\\\ \\mu  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266153 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265440 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266153 1069 "q-132163" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266153 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266153 1071 "Calculate the value of \n$\\,|\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 & -6 & -3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & 10 & 12 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 8 & -8 & -2\\end{aligned}|.$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the right of ${a}_{11}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266153 1072 #uuid "d00f5bdf-fb0f-57e8-b395-12f0570296a6" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266153 1074 "Using elementary column operations, let's make entries to the right of ${a}_{11}=3$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 & -6 & -3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & 10 & 12 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 8 & -8 & -2\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+(-3){C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+2{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+{C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 9 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $1$st row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 9 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 7 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 7 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (3)\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}9 & 7 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =9(18+28) \\\\  & =9(46) \\\\  & =414.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266154 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265479 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266154 1069 "q-132194" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266154 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266154 1071 "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(1,-1)$ where $t=1,$ given that $‖{r}^{′}(1)‖=\\sqrt{13},$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4}},\\,-\\frac{3t}{\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4}}⟩.\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266154 1072 #uuid "3494ae17-5034-59cf-9091-bc1a2a5ea92f" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266154 1074 "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(1)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(1)=⟨1,-1⟩,$ since the point $P(1,-1)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(1)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(1)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3t}{\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{18t}{\\sqrt{{(9{t}^{2}+4)}^{3}}},\\,-\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{{(9{t}^{2}+4)}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{{(9{t}^{2}+4)}^{3}}}⟨-3t,\\,-2⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{{(9({1}^{2})+4)}^{3}}}⟨-3(1),-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{13\\sqrt{13}}⟨-3,-2⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\frac{6}{13\\sqrt{13}}\\cdot \\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{13\\sqrt{13}}\\cdot \\sqrt{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(1) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{(\\frac{6}{13})} \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\sqrt{13}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=1$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(1) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(1)}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}⟨-3,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1)+R(1)N(1) & =⟨1,-1⟩+\\frac{13\\sqrt{13}}{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}⟨-3,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1⟩+\\frac{13}{6}⟨-3,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1⟩+⟨-\\frac{13}{2},-\\frac{13}{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{11}{2},-\\frac{16}{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265420 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266155 1069 "q-132201" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266155 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266155 1071 "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(0,1)$ where $t=0,$ given that $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖=\\sqrt{2},$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266155 1072 #uuid "6665004f-e6b2-5e6c-b764-778c8eb359b7" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266155 1074 "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(0)=⟨0,1⟩,$ since the point $P(0,1)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(0)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=0$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨0,1⟩+\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1⟩+⟨-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265418 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266156 1069 "q-132208" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266156 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266156 1071 "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(3,1)$ where $t=0,$ given that $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖=2,$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}⟩.\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266156 1072 #uuid "256d5ce8-b202-5957-9fe3-266dd8fdafa7" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true] [17592186266156 1074 "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(0)=⟨3,1⟩,$ since the point $P(3,1)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(0)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}},\\,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{({0}^{2}+1)}^{3}}},\\,\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{({0}^{2}+1)}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=0$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=⟨-1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨3,1⟩+2⟨-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,1⟩+⟨-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045585 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0016a292-9097-5977-930d-8d7887754513" 17592186263010 "answer-0029eb5c-a868-5b35-b213-3fa6e3a04a1d" 17592186263011 "answer-005532ea-a569-5e6b-9518-a0150b3fb329" 17592186263012 "answer-00b35a9a-4cdd-5c91-90ee-71d656ed495e" 17592186263013 "answer-00c6448e-4233-5ae7-870b-9f446f54a05a" 17592186263014 "answer-00dfea87-0564-54b8-b2ba-b85b9b81f898" 17592186263015 "answer-0100d61e-bd3a-57c3-ac46-ef0541e36274" 17592186263016 "answer-0107d3b9-5fbb-503f-9f5b-d99973f71407" 17592186263017 "answer-012cd8a6-b675-5154-9601-7a65d5de12d4" 17592186263018 "answer-018e8bd8-204b-52d8-a30a-9f67cdf19eb4" 17592186263019 "answer-01bbaf8f-20a3-58aa-a15a-082f2461eaca" 17592186263020 "answer-01d17947-1f1d-52bd-8878-b1b300a0284c" 17592186263021 "answer-01eb852c-2f90-54fc-91a8-50d23d4e99cf" 17592186263022 "answer-020db8db-6c51-5099-8bcc-a50e3dd281b3" 17592186263023 "answer-02380ca9-1652-569c-aaf3-e861b8bc8ee7" 17592186263024 "answer-02521fb7-e77f-5486-b7ad-fe251be00536" 17592186263025 "answer-02719984-1eed-5b45-abb8-f9d57dc603d6" 17592186263026 "answer-029d5750-b526-536a-a08f-8c172e389abf" 17592186263027 "answer-029edf87-92f0-5f4d-857c-fbcd96d1ad44" 17592186263028 "answer-02b7d490-99e4-5418-8fe2-8ad8a215711f" 17592186263029 "answer-02bdc94f-aecb-598c-8c8e-fd84d09e16d7" 17592186263030 "answer-02c7eef7-3d78-5989-86e0-94ecf6198c5c" 17592186263031 "answer-02d27768-efde-5e9c-8b09-be4578cf7838" 17592186263032 "answer-02e7d909-6673-5927-a334-117f16fa037f" 17592186263033 "answer-031259ca-bfb9-5b7f-b6a8-7ce7b04